<Base title="Know what changed before your robot ships" description="VolnLabs is building tools to help robotics teams establish whether a behavior change is ready to deploy, and whether that evidence still applies to the robot running it. Actryn is the first step: explicit execution and recovery when robot operations are interrupted.">
  <div class="min">
    <main id="main-content">
      <a href="/research#papers" class="pill quiet"><span class="dot"></span>Two papers accepted at NeurIPS 2026 workshops</a>

      <h1>Know what changed before your robot ships<span class="accent">.</span></h1>
      <p class="lead">We’re building VolnLabs to help robotics teams establish whether a behavior change is ready to deploy, and whether that evidence still applies to the robot running it.</p>
      <p class="lead soft">It passed on the test robot. Is the robot in the field still set up the same way, and do we know what happens when something fails?</p>
      <p class="lead">Actryn is our first step: making execution and recovery explicit when robot operations are interrupted.</p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a href="/actryn#run">See Actryn in action</a>
        <a href="/contact" class="secondary">Discuss a release assessment</a>
      </div>

      <h2>The problem</h2>
      <div class="stack">
        <p><strong>A passing test describes one particular setup.</strong> What happens when the controller, the configuration, execution timing or recovery behavior changes?</p>
        <p><strong>Keeping the model unchanged does not keep the behavior unchanged.</strong> Lost calibration, a worn gripper, a degraded cable, a different execution path or a different reset procedure can each change the result.</p>
        <p>A successful run matters. The person who approves a release also needs to know what that run covered. <a href="/the-fix-passed">Read the example: the fix passed. Can we ship it?</a></p>
      </div>

      <h2>The job</h2>
      <div class="stack">
        <p><strong>Getting a robot working again and knowing it is working correctly are two different jobs.</strong></p>
        <p>Establish what changed, what evidence supports the change, and whether the robot running it still matches the setup that evidence covers. Then approve it, reject it or ask for another test.</p>
      </div>

      <h2>Our first step: Actryn</h2>
      <div class="stack">
        <p>Actryn keeps operation identity, authority and recovery explicit when replies are lost or systems restart. A missing reply does not tell you whether the robot moved, so Actryn keeps the outcome unknown unless controller evidence settles it. New work stays blocked until an explicit recovery, and nothing is resubmitted automatically.</p>
        <p>v0.0.1 is a software-only preview. We have reproduced it end to end against a non-actuating controller fixture and published the run’s evidence and limits.</p>
        <p><a href="/actryn#run">See the run, the evidence and the supported environment</a></p>
      </div>

      <h2>Start with one release</h2>
      <p>We’re starting with inspection-robot manufacturers and integrators. Bring one inspection workflow and one proposed behavior change. We’ll take the failure-and-recovery checks your engineers repeat by hand before trusting a robot again, and make them repeatable across every change, on the stack you already run.</p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a href="/contact">Discuss a release assessment</a>
      </div>

      <h2>The research behind it</h2>
      <div class="wide-stack">
        <section class="product-row">
          <div class="meta">Research · continuing</div>
          <div class="stack">
            <h3>AxiomOS</h3>
            <p>A runtime-programmable robot kernel exploring how robots can accept new behavior within explicit limits on authority and physical effects. Actryn applies part of this work at the controller boundary.</p>
            <p><a href="/axiomos">Explore AxiomOS</a></p>
          </div>
        </section>
        <section class="product-row">
          <div class="meta">Papers · 2026</div>
          <div class="stack">
            <h3>Authority and publication for changing behavior</h3>
            <p>Two workshop papers at NeurIPS 2026 on authority lifecycles and transactional publication for agents whose behavior changes at runtime.</p>
            <p><a href="/research#papers">Read the research</a></p>
          </div>
        </section>
      </div>
    </main>
  </div>
</Base>
